{% doc %}
  snippets/icon-mark.liquid

  The **Icon / Shape / Ring** vocabulary, resolved in one place for every block
  that offers it.

  `blocks/_feature-icon.liquid` settled what a mark is in this theme: a glyph, a
  filled shape behind it, and a ring around that. Four blocks now offer the same
  three groups — the Highlight tile, the Accordion row, the Icon with text block
  and the footer's Utility item — and a fifth will want it tomorrow. This is the
  one implementation they share, so "the same settings" means the same code and
  not four copies that drift.

  ## It adds, it does not replace

  Every property below is emitted **only when the merchant has actually chosen
  something**. A block whose Shape and Ring have never been touched writes no
  declarations at all and draws no ring, so its own stylesheet rules are the
  whole answer and the mark looks exactly as it did before this file existed.

  That is what makes the snippet safe to drop into a block that already has a
  design. The caller does not hand over its rules; it threads this vocabulary
  through them as the first fallback:

      background: var(--icon-mark-fill, <whatever it painted before>);

  No specificity contest, no rule to keep in step, and nothing changes until a
  setting is set.

  ## Two calls, one snippet

  `declarations: true` returns the custom property list for the caller's own
  `style` attribute; called without it the snippet returns the ring's `<svg>`,
  to be placed inside the element that carries those properties. Same two-mode
  arrangement as `snippets/spacing.liquid`, and for the same reason: an element
  can only have one `style` attribute, so the caller has to be the one that
  writes it.

  ## The ring is an SVG, not a `border`

  `border-style: dashed` gives no control over the dash — every browser picks
  its own length and gap from the width — and there is no way at all to express
  a round-capped dot. A `<circle>` with `stroke-dasharray` states both, which is
  why **Dash** and **Dash gap** can be settings rather than something a merchant
  has to accept from their browser.

  Dotted is the one style whose spacing is *not* a setting. A dot and the space
  beside it are one proportion, not two numbers — which is exactly why CSS gives
  no control over a dotted border either — so the pitch is twice the thickness
  and Dash gap stays hidden.

  ## The old frame settings still render

  `blocks/badge-highlight.liquid` used to carry `icon_frame_*`: a style, a size,
  a px width, a *percentage* radius, a colour and a background — one control
  doing the work of a Shape and a Ring together. Those ids are read here as the
  fallback beneath the new ones, so a tile saved against the old vocabulary
  draws the mark it always drew: the frame becomes a Shape with a Ring at zero
  offset, which is the same picture by a different name.

  Nothing else in the theme has those keys, so for every other caller the
  translation resolves to nil and costs a comparison.

  @param {object} block - The block's settings hash (`block.settings`)
  @param {boolean} [declarations] - Return the custom properties rather than the ring

  @example
    {%- capture mark -%}{% render 'icon-mark', block: s, declarations: true %}{%- endcapture -%}
    <span class="utility-item__icon icon-mark" style="{{ mark }}">
      {% render 'icon-mark', block: s %}
      {% render 'icon', icon: s.icon, size: icon_size %}
    </span>
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign m = block

  comment
    ---- the frame the old vocabulary drew ----

    Read first and only ever used as a fallback. 50% of a square box is a circle
    and anything under it is a rounded square: that is what the old percentage
    radius meant, so it is what decides the Shape.
  endcomment
  assign frame_style = m.icon_frame_style | default: 'none'
  assign frame_size = m.icon_frame_size | default: 56 | plus: 0
  assign frame_radius_pct = m.icon_frame_radius | default: 50 | plus: 0

  assign frame_shape = ''
  if frame_style != 'none'
    if frame_radius_pct >= 50
      assign frame_shape = 'circle'
    else
      assign frame_shape = 'square'
    endif
  endif
  assign frame_radius_px = frame_size | times: frame_radius_pct | divided_by: 100

  comment
    ---- the shape ----

    `none` is the default in every schema that offers this panel except the
    Feature icon's, whose own design is a circle. That is the one deviation
    between those panels and it is deliberate: a default is the only part of a
    setting that paints something before anybody touches it, so a block that
    already draws its own mark defaults to None and keeps drawing it.

    None means this snippet emits no size, no radius and no fill, and the
    caller's stylesheet is still the whole answer.
  endcomment
  assign shape = m.shape | default: 'none'

  comment
    A Highlight tile saved against `icon_frame_style`. Its Shape reads None
    because that is the schema's default and it was never answered — but the old
    frame says otherwise, and the old frame is what is on the page.

    It is a branch and not a chain of `| default:` filters, and that matters.
    Every new setting now carries the Feature icon's own default — 86px, 11px of
    offset, a 2px ring — so `m.shape_size | default: frame_size` never reaches
    the frame: 86 is not nil. A tile translated field by field would have kept
    its frame's *style* and taken the feature card's *measurements*, which is a
    56px badge redrawn at 86 with a ring floating 11px off it.

    So the two vocabularies are read whole, one or the other, and `from_frame`
    is which.
  endcomment
  assign from_frame = false
  if shape == 'none' and frame_shape != ''
    assign from_frame = true
    assign shape = frame_shape
  endif

  assign shape_chosen = false
  if shape != 'none'
    assign shape_chosen = true
  endif

  if from_frame
    assign shape_size = frame_size
    assign shape_size_mobile = frame_size
  else
    assign shape_size = m.shape_size | default: 86 | plus: 0
    assign shape_size_mobile = m.shape_size_mobile | default: shape_size | plus: 0
  endif

  comment
    A circle is half its own box at every size, so the radius is derived rather
    than asked for — which is why Corner radius belongs to Square and is hidden
    on Circle.
  endcomment
  if shape == 'circle'
    assign shape_radius = shape_size
  elsif from_frame
    assign shape_radius = frame_radius_px
  else
    assign shape_radius = m.shape_radius | default: 16 | plus: 0
  endif

  comment
    ---- the ring ----

    Offset is the clear space between the shape and the ring, and it is taken
    *inward*. The ring is drawn on the mark's own edge and the filled shape
    shrinks by the offset to make the gap, so Shape size is the whole outer
    measurement and nothing ever paints outside it.

    The other direction — the ring growing past the box — is what this used to
    do, and it put an 8px dotted ring over an accordion row's own border. A mark
    that changes the layout around it when a ring is switched on is a mark that
    cannot be dropped into anything.

    A frame drew a line *on* the shape's edge, which is an offset of nothing —
    so a translated tile takes zero here whatever the slider's default says, and
    keeps the thickness the frame was given.
  endcomment
  assign ring_style = m.ring_style | default: 'none'
  if from_frame
    assign ring_style = frame_style
  endif

  assign has_ring = false
  if ring_style != 'none' and shape != 'none'
    assign has_ring = true
  endif

  if from_frame
    assign ring_width = m.icon_frame_width | default: 1 | plus: 0
    assign ring_offset = 0
  else
    assign ring_width = m.ring_width | default: 2 | plus: 0
    assign ring_offset = m.ring_offset | default: 11 | plus: 0
  endif

  comment
    The ring's box is the mark's box, at both breakpoints. Drawn at the desktop
    size with a matching viewBox and stretched by the stylesheet to whatever box
    it lands in, so the dash scales with the ring on a phone rather than the
    desktop dash being wrapped around a smaller circle.
  endcomment
  assign ring_size = shape_size

  comment
    The stroke is centred on the path, so the radius is inset by half the width
    or the ring is clipped by its own viewBox on both sides.
  endcomment
  assign ring_half = ring_size | divided_by: 2.0
  assign ring_radius = ring_size | minus: ring_width | divided_by: 2.0
  assign ring_inset = ring_width | divided_by: 2.0
  assign ring_box = ring_size | minus: ring_width

  comment
    One code path for all three styles, so switching between them cannot change
    anything but the dash. Solid states no pattern at all; dotted is a dash of
    no length under a round cap, which a browser draws as a circle the width of
    the stroke.
  endcomment
  assign ring_dash = ''
  assign ring_cap = 'butt'
  if ring_style == 'dashed'
    assign dash_on = m.ring_dash | default: 2 | plus: 0
    assign dash_off = m.ring_dash_gap | default: 8 | plus: 0
    assign ring_dash = dash_on | append: ' ' | append: dash_off
  elsif ring_style == 'dotted'
    assign dash_off = ring_width | times: 2
    assign ring_dash = '0.01 ' | append: dash_off
    assign ring_cap = 'round'
  endif

  assign ring_spin = m.ring_spin | default: 0 | plus: 0
  if from_frame
    comment
      A frame never turned, so a translated tile does not start turning because
      the new slider ships at 180.
    endcomment
    assign ring_spin = 0
  endif

  comment
    ---- the colours ----

    Each falls back to the frame value it replaces and each is emitted only when
    it holds something, so a mark that never picked a fill keeps whatever its
    own stylesheet paints rather than a colour nobody chose.
  endcomment
  if from_frame
    assign fill_paint = m.icon_frame_background
    assign fill_hover_paint = ''
    assign ring_paint = m.icon_frame_color
    assign ring_hover_paint = ''
  else
    assign fill_paint = m.fill_color
    assign fill_hover_paint = m.fill_hover_color
    assign ring_paint = m.ring_color
    assign ring_hover_paint = m.ring_hover_color
  endif
-%}

{%- if declarations -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Two names per size, resolved into one by `base.css` at the breakpoint.
    Writing the mobile value into the same property is not an option:
    `--x: var(--x-mobile, var(--x))` is a self-reference, which resolves to the
    guaranteed-invalid value and takes the declaration down with it.

    The size, the radius and the fill are gated on the merchant having chosen a
    Shape. Without that the caller is still drawing its own box, and handing it
    a 56px default would resize a mark nobody asked to resize.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if shape_chosen and shape != 'none' -%}
    --icon-mark-size-desktop:{{ shape_size }}px;
    --icon-mark-size-mobile:{{ shape_size_mobile }}px;
    --icon-mark-radius:{{ shape_radius }}px;
    {%- if fill_paint != blank -%}--icon-mark-fill:{{ fill_paint }};{%- endif -%}
    {%- if fill_hover_paint != blank -%}--icon-mark-fill-hover:{{ fill_hover_paint }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if m.icon_color != blank -%}--icon-mark-color:{{ m.icon_color }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- if m.icon_hover_color != blank -%}--icon-mark-color-hover:{{ m.icon_hover_color }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- if has_ring -%}
    {%- if ring_paint != blank -%}--icon-mark-ring-color:{{ ring_paint }};{%- endif -%}
    {%- if ring_hover_paint != blank -%}--icon-mark-ring-color-hover:{{ ring_hover_paint }};{%- endif -%}
    {%- if ring_spin != 0 -%}--icon-mark-ring-spin:{{ ring_spin }}deg;{%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    How far the filled shape is pulled in from the ring. Emitted only when there
    is a ring and a gap to hold open, so a mark with neither keeps its fill
    flush to its own edge.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if has_ring and ring_offset > 0 -%}--icon-mark-inset:{{ ring_offset }}px;{%- endif -%}
{%- elsif has_ring -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Drawn at the desktop size with a matching viewBox, so at that width the dash
    is exactly the length the merchant asked for. The stylesheet stretches it to
    whatever box it is in, so at a smaller size the dash scales with the ring
    rather than the desktop dash being wrapped around a smaller circle.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <svg
    class="icon-mark__ring"
    width="{{ ring_size }}"
    height="{{ ring_size }}"
    viewBox="0 0 {{ ring_size }} {{ ring_size }}"
    fill="none"
    aria-hidden="true"
    focusable="false"
  >
    {%- if shape == 'square' -%}
      <rect
        x="{{ ring_inset }}"
        y="{{ ring_inset }}"
        width="{{ ring_box }}"
        height="{{ ring_box }}"
        rx="{{ shape_radius }}"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="{{ ring_width }}"
        stroke-linecap="{{ ring_cap }}"
        {% if ring_dash != blank %}stroke-dasharray="{{ ring_dash }}"{% endif %}
      />
    {%- else -%}
      <circle
        cx="{{ ring_half }}"
        cy="{{ ring_half }}"
        r="{{ ring_radius }}"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="{{ ring_width }}"
        stroke-linecap="{{ ring_cap }}"
        {% if ring_dash != blank %}stroke-dasharray="{{ ring_dash }}"{% endif %}
      />
    {%- endif -%}
  </svg>
{%- endif -%}
